A recent advisory has been issued regarding a potential cybersecurity risk to network defenders. The risk is associated with the use of HTTPS inspection products, which are designed to monitor and intercept encrypted internet traffic. However, if not properly configured, these products can introduce vulnerabilities into the network. Specifically, if the HTTPS inspection product does not properly validate certificate chains, it can allow attackers to intercept and modify encrypted communications. This can lead to a range of potential attacks, including man-in-the-middle (MitM) attacks and data breaches. To mitigate this risk, network defenders are advised to verify that their HTTPS inspection product properly validates certificate chains, pass any warnings or errors to the client, and use tools like badssl.com to test the product. Additionally, organizations should carefully consider the pros and cons of implementing HTTPS inspection products and take other steps to secure end-to-end communications.

